  "live_records": {
    "q-19176": {
      "dom_id": "step-q19176",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the exact solution to $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)=15.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
