    "q-79392": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79392",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the exact solution to $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)-1=8.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)-1 & =8 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)}=2x-4.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{3}+4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
