    "q-100751": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100751",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=9-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, then what is the value of  $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ (1+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
