    "q-110775": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110775",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3=3,$ what is the value of $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3 & =3 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={e}^{0}=1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
